      body: `## Between two errors

In the nineteenth century, the Catholic Church faced challenges to traditional teaching on faith and reason from two directions. On one side stood **rationalism**, in its various forms—the heirs of the deists, of Kant, and of Hegel—which judged revelation by reason, or reduced it to reason. On the other side stood a Catholic **fideism** or "**traditionalism**," associated with French thinkers such as **Louis Bautain** and **Augustin Bonnetty**, who, alarmed by the skepticism of the Enlightenment, argued that reason alone could know nothing certain about God, and that all religious knowledge depended on a primitive revelation handed down by tradition. Both Bautain (in 1840) and Bonnetty (in 1855) were required to sign statements affirming that reason can prove God's existence.

## Dei Filius

The **First Vatican Council** (1869–1870), better known for defining papal infallibility, also issued, on **24 April 1870**, the dogmatic constitution ***Dei Filius***, "On the Catholic Faith." It addressed directly the relation of faith and reason. Its second chapter declared:

> The same holy mother Church holds and teaches that God, the beginning and end of all things, can be known with certainty by the natural light of human reason from created things; "for the invisible things of him from the creation of the world are clearly seen, being understood by the things that are made" (Romans 1:20).
>
> — First Vatican Council, *Dei Filius*, chapter 2 (1870), in the course's rendering

The council added that revelation is still needed, not only for mysteries beyond reason, but also so that truths reason can know "may be known by everyone with ease, with firm certitude, and with no admixture of error"—repeating, almost word for word, Aquinas's argument from the first question of the *Summa*, which we read in Lesson II.

## Faith as supernatural

The third chapter defined **faith**, in the course's rendering, as "a supernatural virtue by which, with the inspiration and help of God's grace, we believe that what he has revealed is true, not because the intrinsic truth of things is perceived by the natural light of reason, but because of the authority of God himself who reveals it, who can neither deceive nor be deceived." Yet faith is not "a blind movement of the mind": God has added to the inner help of the Spirit external proofs of revelation—miracles and prophecies—which are "most certain signs" suited to the understanding of all. These are Aquinas's motives of credibility.

## No conflict

The fourth chapter stated the principle of harmony:

> Even though faith is above reason, there can never be any real disagreement between faith and reason, since it is the same God who reveals the mysteries and infuses faith, and who has endowed the human mind with the light of reason.
>
> — *Dei Filius*, chapter 4, in the course's rendering

Apparent conflicts arise either because the teachings of faith have been misunderstood, or because the opinions of reason have been mistaken for conclusions. Faith and reason, the council said, help each other: reason demonstrates the foundations of faith and, enlightened by faith, develops the science of divine things; faith frees and protects reason from errors.

## Canons

To the chapters were attached **canons** condemning opposing views. The first canon of chapter 2 condemned anyone who said that "the one true God, our creator and lord, cannot be known with certainty from the things that have been made, by the natural light of human reason." Notice the precise claim: that God *can* be known by reason, not that anyone in particular *has* proved it, or that a particular proof works.

## After the council

In 1879, Leo XIII's *Aeterni Patris* commended Aquinas as the model for Catholic philosophy, as we saw in Lesson II. In 1910, the oath against modernism required Catholic clergy to profess that God "can be known with certainty and can also be demonstrated" by reason. For the next half century, Catholic theology was dominated by a confident neo-scholastic natural theology. Critics, including many Catholic theologians after the Second Vatican Council, found this approach too rationalistic, and too remote from Scripture and experience. *Fides et Ratio*, as we will see, tried to recover the council's teaching in a new key.

> **Pause and consider.** *Dei Filius* teaches that God *can* be known by reason, not that any particular proof succeeds. Why might the council have stated the claim so carefully?`,
