      title: "Wittgenstein and the Language-Games of Faith",
      body: `## The mystical

The Austrian philosopher **Ludwig Wittgenstein** (1889–1951) influenced the debate about religion in two very different ways. In his early ***Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus*** (1921), which inspired the logical positivists, he argued that meaningful language pictures facts in the world. Ethics, aesthetics, and the meaning of life cannot be said; they lie outside the world. Yet Wittgenstein did not think them unimportant. Near the end of the *Tractatus*, he wrote:

> Not how the world is, is the mystical, but that it is.
>
> — Ludwig Wittgenstein, *Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus* 6.44, trans. C. K. Ogden (1922)

And the book ends: "Whereof one cannot speak, thereof one must be silent" (7). The positivists read this silence as dismissal; Wittgenstein meant it, many think, as reverence.

## Language-games

In his later philosophy, published after his death as *Philosophical Investigations* (1953), Wittgenstein rejected the picture theory. The meaning of a word, he argued, is its **use** in a particular **language-game**, embedded in a **form of life**. Language does not do just one thing, picture facts; it does many things—commanding, praying, joking, thanking, cursing, greeting. To understand a word, we must look at how it is used, not assume that all language works like scientific description.

## Lectures on religious belief

In lectures given at Cambridge around 1938, published from students' notes in 1966, Wittgenstein applied these ideas to religion. Suppose, he said, someone believes in a Last Judgment, and I do not. Do I contradict him? Not in the way I would contradict someone who said there was a German aeroplane overhead. The believer's belief is not a hypothesis held tentatively on evidence, which might be overturned by further evidence. It is something that "regulates" his whole life, that shows itself in how he lives. If someone said they believed in the Last Judgment as a well-supported forecast, held on a certain degree of probability, Wittgenstein suggested, we would think them confused about what religious belief is. Religious belief, on this view, is neither reasonable nor unreasonable in the way scientific hypotheses are; it belongs to a different language-game.

## D. Z. Phillips

The Welsh philosopher **D. Z. Phillips** (1934–2006) developed these ideas in many books, including *The Concept of Prayer* (1965) and *Death and Immortality* (1970). He argued that religious concepts have their own **grammar**: to understand "God," "prayer," or "eternal life," one must see how they function in the life of believing communities. Prayer is not an attempt to influence a powerful being to change the course of events, but an expression of dependence and of love. Eternal life is not a continuation of existence after death, but a quality of life lived in relation to God. Critics of religion, Phillips argued, typically misunderstand religious language by treating it as quasi-scientific; but so do many defenders of religion.

## Wittgensteinian fideism

In 1967, the Canadian philosopher **Kai Nielsen** published an article titled "**Wittgensteinian Fideism**," which gave this approach its name. Nielsen argued that it insulated religion from criticism: if each form of life has its own internal standards of rationality, then no outsider can criticize religious belief, and believers cannot say that their beliefs are true in any sense that matters to unbelievers. Moreover, he argued, ordinary believers think that God really exists, that prayer really is addressed to someone, and that there really is life after death; an account that denies this does not describe religious belief, but revises it.

## Assessment

Wittgensteinian approaches have helped philosophers see how varied religious language is, and how far it is from the abstract hypotheses that both believers and critics sometimes debate. They echo the medieval insight, in Pseudo-Dionysius and Aquinas, that God is not one object among others. But most philosophers, theistic and atheistic alike, agree with Nielsen that religious beliefs typically involve claims about reality that can be true or false, and so cannot escape the questions of evidence and rationality.

> **Pause and consider.** Wittgenstein suggested that a person who believed in the Last Judgment on the basis of probability would misunderstand religious belief. What did he mean? Is he right?`,
